Root Causes of the >10 min Timeout
1. Test-FileLocked — Biggest Culprit
Old behavior: Safe-RemoveFiles opened a FileStream for every single file to test if it was locked before deleting. On a Citrix server with dozens of active user profiles, this means tens of thousands of file-open operations, one by one.
Fix: Removed Test-FileLocked entirely. Remove-Item -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue already skips locked files silently — no stream-open check needed. This alone can cut runtime by 60–70%.

2. C:\Users* Wildcard Expanded 9 Times Separately
Old behavior: Safe-RemoveFiles was called 9 times with C:\Users\*\AppData\... paths. Each call re-enumerated C:\Users\* from scratch and then recursed into subdirectories — that's 9 full profile tree walks.
Fix: New code enumerates C:\Users once, then loops through all 9 sub-paths per profile. One pass instead of nine.

3. CleanMgr.exe -Wait — No Timeout
Old behavior: Start-Process CleanMgr.exe -Wait blocks forever if cleanmgr hangs (common on Citrix/RDS).
Fix: Replaced with -PassThru + WaitForExit(120000). If cleanmgr doesn't finish in 2 minutes, it's killed and the script continues. Fully configurable via $global:CleanMgrTimeoutSec.

4. Stop-Service / Start-Service — No Timeout
Old behavior: If wuauserv or TrustedInstaller was stuck, Stop-Service could block indefinitely.
Fix: New Stop-ServiceSafe / Start-ServiceSafe functions poll the service status and give up after 30 seconds, then log a warning and continue.

5. No Overall Script Budget
Old behavior: No ceiling — if any phase ran long, the whole script ran long.
Fix: Added Assert-TimeRemaining called before every major phase. If total elapsed time exceeds 8 minutes ($global:ScriptTimeoutSec = 480), the script exits cleanly with code 0 (not a failure). Adjust this value to match your automation platform's timeout setting.

